[1m• [0mThe codebase is clear. The package is named [36mimp[0m (version [36m0.0.1[0m in
  [36mpyproject.toml[0m), and the CLI is a single [36margparse[0m parser in
  [36msrc/imp/cli/main.py[0m with [36mrequired=True[0m subparsers.

  Here's my proposed design:

  [1m • [0mAdd [36mparser.add_argument("--version", "-V", action="version", version=f"imp [0m
  [1m   [0m[36m{<version>}")[0m to the [1mtop-level[0m parser in [36mmain()[0m. Argparse's [36mversion[0m action
  [1m   [0mfires during [36mparse_args[0m and exits 0 [3mbefore[0m the required-subparser check, so
  [1m   [0m[36mimp --version[0m / [36mimp -V[0m work with no subcommand — no special-casing needed.
  [1m • [0mRead the version via a small helper using
  [1m   [0m[36mimportlib.metadata.version("imp")[0m.
  [1m • [0mOutput format: [36mimp 0.0.1[0m (one line, to stdout, exit 0) — the argparse
  [1m   [0m[36mversion[0m action default.
  [1m • [0mAdd a CLI test asserting [36mimp --version[0m and [36mimp -V[0m print the metadata
  [1m   [0mversion and exit 0.

  One decision worth your call: [1mif the package metadata can't be found[0m
  ([36mPackageNotFoundError[0m — e.g. running from a source checkout that was never
  installed), should [36m--version[0m (a) print a fallback like [36mimp unknown[0m and still
  exit 0, or (b) let it error out? I lean toward (a) so [36m--version[0m never crashes.

  Does this look right, and which fallback do you want?
